Article 10

(1) The Regional Government is tasked with carrying out efforts to prevent violence against women and children, in the form of:

a. collecting data and information on women and children victims of acts of violence, and laws and regulations;
b. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the implementation of protection for women and children;
c. conducting education on anti-violence values toward women and children; and
d. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection for women and children.
(2) The Regional Government in anticipating acts of violence against women and children, is authorized to provide and organize services for victims in the form of:
a. providing and facilitating the establishment of integrated services for victims of acts of violence by involving institutions and community elements;
b. encouraging community awareness of the importance of protection for victims; and
c. conducting community empowerment for the prevention of acts of violence.

Article 16

(1) To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government conducts empowerment and awareness to families, parents, and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling.

(2) In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government undertakes the following efforts:

a. increasing the quantity and quality of education, both formal and non-formal and informal;
b. opening accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. opening employment opportunities for women;
d. building community participation and concern to implement prevention and protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. building and providing a complete and easily accessible information system;
f. implementing curfews for children (under 18 years) leaving the house at night no later than 11.00 pm, if beyond that time must be accompanied by parents or family;
g. restricting children from accessing the internet at internet cafes (warnet) during school hours;
h. building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children; and
i. opening complaint posts for the protection of women and children from acts of violence.

Article 17

(1) Prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 16, is carried out by SKPD whose duties and functions are in the field of:

Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Children

a. social;

b. health;

c. education;

d. manpower;

e. women's empowerment and child protection;

f. mental and spiritual; and

g. tranquility and order.

(2) Prevention of acts of violence by SKPD as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on the Regional Action Plan.

Article 3

(1) The objectives of implementing the protection of women and children are to: a. prevent the occurrence of acts of violence against women and children; b. eliminate all forms of violence and exploitation against women and children;

c. protect and provide a sense of security for women and children; d. provide services to women and children, reporters, and witnesses;

Perda Kab. HSU Tahun 2016 Nomor 3 ttg Perlindungan Perempuan dan Anak

e. empower women for the fulfillment of the rights of women and children; and

f. facilitate and mediate household disputes to realize a harmonious and prosperous household integrity.

(2) The objectives of implementing the protection of women and children as referred to in paragraph (1), include aspects of:

a. prevention;
b. service and assistance;
c. reunification;
d. empowerment.

Article 24

(1) In order to achieve the objectives of implementing protection for women and children from acts of violence as referred to in Article 3, the Regional Government cooperates with:

a. the government;
b. the provincial government;
c. other regency/city governments;
d. universities; and
e. non-governmental organizations.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1), includes: a. exchange of data and information;
b. rehabilitation of victims of violence;
c. repatriation and social reintegration; and
d. provision of evidence and witnesses, and followed up in accordance with applicable law.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2), is set forth in the form of joint agreement.

Article 22

(1) The Regional Government is obliged to conduct empowerment of women victims of violence.

(2) Empowerment of victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), is carried out by P2TP2A.

(3) In conducting empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government formulates empowerment programs in the economic field, which include:

a. procuring the necessary needs for entrepreneurship training for women, in order to increase knowledge, attitudes, and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises, as well as financial institutions to develop women's businesses;
e. seeking to provide capital for women victims of violence; and Regional Regulation of HSU Regency Year 2016 Number 3 concerning Protection of Women and Children
f. expanding information access and promoting women's product results.
(4) In conducting empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government may establish cooperation with job training institutions organized by the private sector.

Article 33

(1) In addition to general investigative officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation, Perda Kab. HSU Tahun 2016 Nomor 3 ttg Perlindungan Perempuan dan Anak may also be conducted by Civil Servant Investigators ( PPNS ) as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Code.

(2) PPNS as referred to in paragraph (1), are certain civil servant officials within the Regional Government who are appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

(3) In carrying out investigative duties, PPNS as referred to in paragraph (2), are authorized to:

a. receive reports or complaints from persons regarding criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take initial action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. seize objects and/or documents;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in experts required in connection with the examination of cases;
h. discontinue investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify the Public Prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other necessary actions for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
(4) PPNS as referred to in paragraph (2), notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator Official of the Indonesian National Police, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.

