Article 33

(1) In addition to general investigative officials, investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children in this Regional Regulation, Perda Kab. HSU Tahun 2016 Nomor 3 ttg Perlindungan Perempuan dan Anak may also be conducted by Civil Servant Investigators ( PPNS ) as referred to in the Criminal Procedure Code.

(2) PPNS as referred to in paragraph (1), are certain civil servant officials within the Regional Government who are appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

(3) In carrying out investigative duties, PPNS as referred to in paragraph (2), are authorized to:

a. receive reports or complaints from persons regarding criminal acts of violence against women and children;
b. take initial action at that time at the scene and conduct examination;
c. interrogate a suspect and examine the suspect's identification;
d. seize objects and/or documents;
e. take fingerprints and photograph a person;
f. summon persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bring in experts required in connection with the examination of cases;
h. discontinue investigation after receiving instructions from the General Investigator that there is insufficient evidence, or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the General Investigator notify the Public Prosecutor, the suspect or their family; and/or
i. take other necessary actions for the smooth investigation of criminal acts of violence against women and children, which according to law can be accounted for.
(4) PPNS as referred to in paragraph (2), notify the commencement of investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator Official of the Indonesian National Police, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.

Article 14

Forms of services for victims organized by P2TP2A include:

a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical and paramedical personnel;
b. medicolegal services constitute a form of medical service for the purposes of evidence in the legal field;
c. psychosocial services constitute services provided by companions in order to restore the traumatic condition of victims, including the provision of safe houses to protect victims from various threats and intimidation for victims and provide social support so that victims have confidence, strength and independence in resolving their problems;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing judicial processes;
e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and may provide economic access so that victims can be independent.

